<LessonLayout title="Catechist's Guide &mdash; The Morality of Human Acts" cic="CCC 1749-1761" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Catechist's Guide</h2>
<p class="big-question">For preparing and leading the class.</p>

<h3>Class Summary</h3>

<p><strong>Estimated time:</strong> 60 minutes</p>
<p><strong>Core topic:</strong> The morality of human acts depends on three sources: the object chosen, the intention, and the circumstances. All three must be good for an act to be morally good.</p>
<p><strong>Objectives:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Understand the three sources of morality for human acts.</li>
<li>Distinguish between intrinsically evil acts and acts that depend on circumstances.</li>
<li>Recognize that a good intention cannot justify an evil act.</li>
<li>Understand the principle of double effect and its limits.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key verse:</strong> &laquo;The tree is known by its fruit&raquo; (Mt 12:33)</p>
<p><strong>Catechism references:</strong> CCC 1749-1761</p>

</section>

