<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<p><strong>Key teaching:</strong> The three-source framework of Catholic morality is one of its greatest strengths. It avoids two extremes: the rigorism that only looks at the external act (object alone), and the relativism that says &laquo;only intentions matter&raquo;.</p>

<p><strong>Intrinsically evil acts:</strong> Emphasize that some acts are always wrong. This is not &laquo;rigid&raquo; but rather protects human dignity. The Church teaches this with confidence because it flows from the natural law, knowable by reason, not just by faith.</p>

<p><strong>The end does not justify the means:</strong> This is perhaps the most important moral principle to convey. Our culture constantly says otherwise. Give strong, clear examples.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>&#x1F331; Seed (Ages 7-12)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Simplify: &laquo;God sees not just what we do, but why we do it.&raquo;</li>
  <li>Activity: draw a tree with &laquo;good fruit&raquo; and &laquo;bad fruit&raquo; actions.</li>
  <li>Avoid double effect. Focus on the three sources.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1F33F; Sprout (Ages 13-17)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Connect to their world: social media, peer pressure, &laquo;everyone does it&raquo;.</li>
  <li>Discuss real scenarios: &laquo;Is it ok to cheat if my family needs me to pass?&raquo;</li>
  <li>Double effect as a tool for thinking about complex situations.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1FAB4; Root (Adults new in faith)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>May have a legalistic or a permissive background. Help them find the middle.</li>
  <li>Emphasize that God cares about the whole person, not just rule-keeping.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1F333; Tree (Formed adults)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Explore the principle of double effect in bioethics (end-of-life care, pregnancy complications).</li>
  <li>Compare with consequentialist ethics (Utilitarianism) and why the Church rejects it.</li>
</ul>

</section>

