<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<p><strong>1. &laquo;Is it always wrong to lie? Even to save a life?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>Yes, the act of lying is intrinsically evil (CCC 2484-2485). But you are not obligated to reveal the truth to someone who has no right to it. You may remain silent, deflect, or state what you have a right to say. The classic example: hiding Jews in your home during the Holocaust. You don&rsquo;t need to lie &mdash; you can say &laquo;there is no one here&raquo; meaning &laquo;no one here who you have a right to find&raquo; (mental reservation).</p>

<p><strong>2. &laquo;Isn&rsquo;t the Catholic approach too complicated?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>Moral life is complex because life is complex. The Church gives us principles, not a checklist. The more you form your conscience, the more natural it becomes to discern well. It&rsquo;s like learning a language: at first every word is hard, but eventually it flows.</p>

<p><strong>3. &laquo;What if I didn&rsquo;t know it was wrong?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>If you truly could not have known (invincible ignorance), you are not culpable. But you have a responsibility to form your conscience. The question is: did you choose not to know?</p>

</section>

